  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 1238 AM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nThe winds turning to the SE at the end of the short-term is a \nsignal for the next broader pattern change. While the subtropical \nridge maintains a good hold over TX, undercutting it will be a \ndeepening tropical flow. This pattern is being accelerated \nslightly, thanks to a shift in the track of now Hurricane Simon \nover the Eastern Pacific. Its more direct charge toward the Coast \nof Mexico appears to be having an influence on the moisture influx\ninto TX for early next week. High temperatures Sunday may be a in\nlower confidence as this moisture arrives and probably mixes out,\nbut by Monday, a deeper moist layer could get established, and \nthe mild morning lows return to the warm and muggy conditions seen\nin September. The highs are also expected to still punch into the\n90s for many areas, so there could be some heat index values \npushing toward triple digits again by Monday afternoon. \n\nOn a more positive note, the increasing moisture trends, as \ncompared to the model runs of yesterday, reveal a bit more \ncoverage of showers and storms, with perhaps some good seabreeze \nactivity arriving in the afternoon. This tendency gets our \nforecast area into a holding pattern as whats left of Simon gets \npulled across the high terrain of Mexico, missing us mainly to the\nnorthwest. Not having a strong surface feature close at hand, \nwe'll probably miss out on one of those good deep convection \nevents commonly associated with Pacific cyclones. However, with \nthe sharp topography over the west coast likely destroying Simon \nrather quickly, the fragments of the remnants could get hung up in\nMexico for several days, leading to a potential heavy rain event \nwhen our next cold front is suggested to arrive about 9 days out. \nThis very thing occurred in 2015 where TC Patricia generated not 1\nbut 2 well separated heavy rain events over the area from two \ndifferent cold fronts. So for now the extended forecast continues \nwith heat, humidity, and some spotty rains, but the next big \npattern change may be just over the horizon.",
